/**
@mainpage FLARE Framework Reference

English API documentation for the current C++ framework under `src/`.
Function comments describe purpose, parameters, return values, required input
conditions, exceptions, and relevant side effects. They document the current
implementation rather than planned functionality.

@section doc_navigation Reading the API

- @ref hd_core "hd_core": primitive and conserved states, cells, and mesh geometry/indexing.
- @ref hydro "hydro": reconstruction, slope limiters, Riemann solvers, spatial RHS, and CFL limits.
- @ref hd_time "hd_time": forward Euler, SSP-RK2, source-aware RHS evaluation, and timestep dispatch.
- @ref boundary "boundary": ghost-cell filling and user boundary callbacks.
- @ref hd_source "hd_source": source registration, constant gravity, constant heating, and geometric sources.
- @ref config "config": parameter parsing, validation, and mesh construction.
- @ref io "io": terminal diagnostics and CSV snapshots.
- @ref hd_user "hd_user": problem-specific initialization, boundary, and output callbacks.
- @ref hdmath "hdmath" and @ref utils "utils": mathematical vectors and string comparison.

Use the class, namespace, and file navigation or the search field to locate an
interface. Header declarations carry the public documentation. Internal helper
functions are documented in their implementation files; source links are included.

@section source_parameters Source-specific parameters

`SourceFunction` accepts ordinary functions and lambdas with owned captures.
Keep parameters in a source-specific struct and capture them by value in a
factory returning `SourceTerm`. The implementation can take extra typed parameters;
the factory adapts it to the common `(mesh, time, cfg, rhs, dt_limit)` interface.
See `make_constant_gravity` and `constant_gravity` for a complete example.

@code{.cpp}
hd_source::ConstantGravityParams params;
params.acceleration[0] = -1.0;
hd_source::SourceList sources;
sources.push_back(hd_source::make_constant_gravity(params));
// A scalar parameter, captured by value:
sources.push_back(hd_source::make_constant_heating(0.5));
// For a supported non-Cartesian mesh:
sources.push_back(hd_source::make_geometric_source());
@endcode

Normal simulations use `make_sources(cfg, mesh, hooks)`, which still reads the
existing gravity/heating settings and automatically registers the geometric source.
It then calls the optional `hooks.register_sources(cfg, mesh, sources)` callback.
Define that callback in the problem code and append any number of custom sources.
Include `source/source.h` in the implementation that constructs or appends sources;
`user/user_api.h` only forward-declares `SourceTerm` and uses a reference to
`std::vector<SourceTerm>`, which is the same type as `SourceList`.
Gravity/heating parameters are copied when the source is created; rebuild it to change
the acceleration or heating rate. The geometric source uses the current stage mesh and currently
implements spherical 1D. Custom parameter-file parsing is still the responsibility
of the new source's configuration code.

Enable built-in heating with `constant_heating_enabled = true` and `heating_rate = 0.5`
in the simulation parameter file. The rate is energy per unit mass per unit time:
`dE/dt = rho * heating_rate`. The source limits dt to 0.1 times the shortest active-cell
heating timescale `internal_energy / (rho * heating_rate)`, using internal rather than
total energy. The safety factor is fixed; the source accepts only one double parameter.
Zero rate and vacuum add no heating and impose no timestep limit. Existing simulations
default to heating disabled. A user heating source registered at the same time adds
its own contribution; avoid enabling both when comparing the two implementations.

Callbacks add rates without multiplying by dt and may only tighten dt_limit.
RK2 can retry stages: use the supplied mesh and stage time, and avoid committing
physical state in captured variables during evaluation. The integrators pass the
source list by const reference, without copying captured data each step.

@section performance_profiling Runtime performance measurements

Rebuild the problem, then enable optional serial wall-clock profiling:
@code{.sh}
./flare -par parameters.txt --performance
./flare -par parameters.txt --performance-detail --performance-file performance.json
@endcode

With neither option, profiling is disabled: scopes do not read the clock or allocate,
and no report is printed. The numerical algorithms and output cadence are unchanged.
`--performance-file PATH` implies summary profiling unless detail mode is selected.
Paths are relative to the working directory; the parent directory must exist.
The text report goes to stderr, separately from the existing stdout diagnostics:
@code{.sh}
./flare -par parameters.txt --performance > run.log 2> performance.log
@endcode

Summary mode measures initialization, evolution, time advancement, RHS, hydro,
face fluxes, flux divergence, sources, boundaries, state updates, mesh copies,
whole-mesh validation, and output. Detail mode additionally times every face
reconstruction and Riemann call. Use summary mode for overall throughput;
the two clock reads and bookkeeping for each detailed scope can noticeably
perturb a hot loop. Neither mode subtracts estimated instrumentation overhead.
Measurements are elapsed wall time, including I/O and scheduling delays, not CPU time.

`Inclusive(s)` includes nested instrumented operations. `Self(s)` subtracts those
child scopes; `Self %` divides self time by the whole run's elapsed time. Sum self
times, not inclusive times. A section may be called from several parents, and its
row aggregates those calls. The whole run starts before parameter loading and
ends after simulation cleanup/stdout flush; CLI parsing and report generation are
outside it. Evolution includes per-step diagnostics and snapshots. Initialization
includes mesh/model loading, user initialization, the first boundary fill and
initial output. The validation row covers instrumented whole-mesh validation;
per-cell validation inside other operations remains charged to those operations.

Output is split into calls that write snapshots (including their diagnostics and
user output hook) and calls that only produce diagnostics. It is not a measurement
of pure disk-writing time, and lowering snapshot frequency does not remove the
code's per-step diagnostics. A source callback without its own timer is included
in the self time of `sources`.

Counters record accepted steps, accepted active-cell updates (not RK stages),
RK2 attempts/rejections, and successful snapshot output calls. Failed/rejected
work is included in timings; failed scopes still count as calls. A caught simulation
exception emits a partial report with status `failed` and preserves the failure
exit code. SIGKILL/abrupt termination cannot produce a final report. JSON contains
schema_version, status, mode, clock, wall_seconds, counters, and section timings.

`utils/performance.h` provides RAII `performance::Scope` and `measure()` wrappers.
Use existing section IDs, or add a named enum entry and its label for a new category.
Scopes must close in reverse construction order, including explicit `stop()` calls.
The current profiler is serial and not thread-safe; thread-parallel hydro would
require per-thread accounting, just as its current global wave-speed record does.

Regression checks:
@code{.sh}
make -C unit_test -j4 test
make -C tests/sod_tube -j4
make -C tests/File_Profile_2D -j4
make -C tests/CCSN_MESA_day13 -j4
python3 tests/performance_checks.py
@endcode

The integration checks use temporary outputs, compare off/summary/detail physics
files and stdout byte for byte, validate accounting and CLI error handling, and
write reports under tests/initialization_validation/output/performance. Optional
`--baseline-dir DIR` also compares binaries named CASE.baseline from before the change.

@subsection optimized_hydro Optimized conversions, reconstruction and exact pressure solves

State conversions compute velocity norms directly from fixed-size state arrays;
valid conversions no longer allocate temporary vectors. Each hydro RHS owns a fresh
lazy primitive/slope cache, so neighboring faces reuse cell conversions and PLM
slopes. Only stencil cells are read; unused multidimensional ghost corners are not
converted. RK stages/retries get new caches. Nonuniform spacing, per-cell gamma,
limiters and independent positivity fallbacks retain their previous arithmetic.
PLM cache storage is approximately `(NDIM+1)*(sizeof(Primitives)+1)` bytes per
stored cell plus small per-axis geometry arrays; it is released with that RHS.

Select `reconstructor = PLM_corrected` to enable positive face reconstruction;
`limiter = MC` (or any existing limiter) still controls the initial slopes.
After limiting, a common factor in [0,1] scales all hydrodynamic primitive slopes
for each cell and direction so both faces retain at least 1e-10 of the center
density and pressure, up to roundoff. Corrected slopes are cached once per RHS.
Unconstrained slopes are unchanged. PCM/PLM and their defaults are unchanged.
The existing validation fallback remains for vacuum/numerical exceptions.
This is a spatial discretization change at constrained faces, not a guarantee
of positive cell averages after time integration or of Newton convergence.
It introduces no new runtime parameter besides the reconstruction name.
Run `make -C unit_test -j4 TESTS=reconstruction` for 1D/2D/3D checks.

`riemann_solver = exact` now uses analytic-derivative Newton iteration with a
positive pressure bracket. Side coefficients are cached per face. A bounded acoustic
initial guess speeds smooth flow; out-of-bracket/nonfinite proposals and every eighth
iteration use bisection. Convergence still requires a sign-changing pressure interval
whose width is at most `riemann_tolerance * upper_pressure`. When a Newton step is
small, the solver explicitly tests a narrow interval around the trial pressure;
it does not accept a small residual alone. The original iteration cap, failure
exceptions, vacuum handling and exact interface sampling remain in force.
Roundoff-level output changes are expected because the root algorithm changed.
Use `riemann_solver = exact_bisection` to select the retained original pressure
algorithm for reproducibility/comparison; no other parameter changes are needed.

Both profiling modes include `exact_roots` statistics (without per-iteration clocks):
number of gas/gas pressure solves, total/maximum iterations, residual evaluations,
initial guesses, accepted Newton steps, bisection steps, bracket expansions,
generated-vacuum solves and failures. Initial guesses plus Newton and bisection
steps sum to iterations. Residual evaluations include bracketing and convergence
checks, but exclude the final two wave evaluations used for contact velocity.
The displayed mean includes all pressure solves, including generated vacuum at
zero iterations; initially empty sides bypass pressure solving. JSON also includes
iteration histogram buckets `0, 1-4, 5-8, 9-16, 17-32, 33-64, 65+`.

@code{.sh}
make -C unit_test -j4 TESTS=optimization
python3 tests/optimization_checks.py --repeats 3
# Optional original binaries named CASE.baseline:
python3 tests/optimization_checks.py --baseline-dir /path/to/baselines
@endcode

These checks compare bisection output byte for byte when original binaries are
provided, compare final states at identical physical times for Newton, verify
finite/nonnegative states and root accounting, and record repeated wall times.
Velocity errors are scaled by at least the reference maximum sound speed; density
and pressure errors use the respective reference maximum. Reports are written to
`tests/initialization_validation/output/optimization`. Timing runs should be serial
with no concurrent builds or simulations. MESA uses 0.01 physical seconds here;
this is a short regression/throughput test, not validation of a full explosion.

@section doc_build Building the documentation

Install Doxygen, then run from the repository root:
@code{.sh}
doxygen Doxyfile
@endcode

Open `build/docs/html/index.html`. Doxygen writes diagnostics to
`build/docs/doxygen-warnings.log` and machine-readable documentation to
`build/docs/xml/`. The generated files stay under the ignored `build/` directory.
This build does not compile or run a simulation and does not publish a website.
*/
